<p>Website copy that ranks and converts does two jobs at once. It uses the words your customers actually type into Google, so the page shows up, and then it answers their question fast and gives them one clear next step, so they call, book or buy. The simplest way to get there is to give every page one search to answer and one action to ask for.</p>

<p>Most small business websites miss one side or the other. Some read like a keyword list and nobody wants to contact them. Others sound lovely but never say what the business does in words anyone searches for. Here's how to write copy that does both, page by page.</p>

<h2>Start with what your customer is searching for</h2>

<p>Before you write a word, figure out the question each page answers. Think about what someone in Albuquerque or Rio Rancho would type when they need you. It's rarely your company name or a clever tagline. It's things like "emergency AC repair," "wedding venue in the mountains" or "how to let go of negative thoughts."</p>

<p>A few easy ways to find those words:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Listen to your customers.</strong> The questions people ask on the phone, in emails and at your counter are often word for word what they search.</li>
  <li><strong>Type your service into Google.</strong> Look at the suggestions in the search bar and the "People also ask" box. Those are real searches.</li>
  <li><strong>Check Search Console.</strong> If your site is connected to Google Search Console, the search results report shows the searches already sending you visitors.</li>
</ul>

<p>Then match one main search to one page. Your homepage covers who you are and where you work. Each service gets its own page. Common questions get their own posts. When two pages chase the same search, they end up competing with each other.</p>

<p>Vince likes to say marketing strategy isn't the fancy clothes on top of your company, it's the skeleton underneath. Copy works the same way. If you haven't nailed down who you're talking to and what makes you different, no amount of clever wording will hold the page up.</p>

<h2>Put the search words where Google and people look first</h2>

<p>Once you know the search a page answers, use those words in the spots that matter most:</p>

<ol>
  <li><strong>The page title.</strong> This is the blue link in search results. Google's <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/appearance/title-link" target="_blank" rel="noopener">title link guidelines</a> ask for titles that are descriptive and concise, unique to each page, and free of keyword stuffing. "Home" or your business name alone wastes the spot.</li>
  <li><strong>The main headline.</strong> It can be bold and creative, but the subheading or first line should say plainly what the page is about.</li>
  <li><strong>The first paragraph.</strong> Answer the question right away. People decide in seconds whether they're in the right place.</li>
  <li><strong>Headings.</strong> Phrase them the way people ask. "How much does a roof inspection take?" beats "Our Process."</li>
</ol>

<p>Write naturally. Say it the way you'd say it to a customer, then use related words the way a person would. Repeating "best plumber Albuquerque" in every sentence reads badly to people and looks spammy to Google.</p>

<h2>A real example: a bold headline plus plain words</h2>

<p>When we built the website for Lumanta, a thought-awareness app, we added three pages, one for each step of the app's method: Catch, Shift and Rise. Each page has a big, emotional headline that matches the brand, like "Notice it. Let it go." Right under it sits a plain subheading that answers a real search: "How to let go of negative thoughts." The first paragraph then explains it in simple language.</p>

<figure class="inline-img"><img src="/images/work/lumanta-screens/desktop-04.webp" alt="Lumanta homepage section with the headline A mindfulness app for negative thoughts, next to a short description and the lines No journaling. No analyzing. No judging yourself." width="1440" height="812" loading="lazy" decoding="async"><figcaption>The Lumanta homepage says what the app is in plain words: a mindfulness app for negative thoughts.</figcaption></figure>

<p>That's the balance to aim for. The headline makes people feel something. The words right below it tell Google and the reader exactly what the page covers. You can see the whole project in our <a href="/work/lumanta">Lumanta case study</a>.</p>

<h2>Write for people who scan</h2>

<p>Most visitors don't read a web page top to bottom. They scan headings, the start of paragraphs and anything bold, looking for the part that answers their question. The <a href="https://www.nngroup.com/videos/f-pattern-reading-digital-content/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Nielsen Norman Group's research on the F-pattern</a> shows how readers skim down the left side of a page and read less as they go.</p>

<p>So make scanning easy:</p>

<ul>
  <li>Lead each section and paragraph with the most important point.</li>
  <li>Keep paragraphs to two to four sentences.</li>
  <li>Use lists for steps, options and what's included.</li>
  <li>Bold a few key phrases, not whole paragraphs.</li>
  <li>Check the page on your phone. A paragraph that looks short on a laptop can fill a whole phone screen.</li>
</ul>

<h2>Be specific, and say what only you can say</h2>

<p>Vague copy is the biggest reason good businesses don't stand out. "Quality service you can trust" could belong to anyone. Specific details give people, and search engines, a reason to pick you:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>What exactly you do</strong>, in the words customers use.</li>
  <li><strong>Who it's for</strong> and the areas you serve, like Albuquerque, Santa Fe or the East Mountains.</li>
  <li><strong>How it works</strong>, step by step, so people know what to expect.</li>
  <li><strong>Proof</strong>, like real photos of your team and work, customer reviews quoted word for word, and examples of past projects.</li>
</ul>

<p>Google's guide to <a href="https://developers.google.com/search/docs/fundamentals/creating-helpful-content" target="_blank" rel="noopener">creating helpful, reliable, people-first content</a> makes the same point: content should come from real expertise and first-hand experience, and should leave a reader feeling they got what they came for. That's also why copy that only rewords what's already online struggles. If AI or a template could have written your page for any business in any city, it won't give anyone a reason to choose yours. We dig into that more in <a href="/blog/ai-generated-website-vs-custom-design">AI website vs. custom design</a>, and real photos get their own post in <a href="/blog/real-photos-vs-stock-photos-website">why real photos beat stock photos</a>.</p>

<h2>Turn readers into calls with one clear next step</h2>

<p>Ranking only gets people to the page. Converting is about what happens next. A few habits make a big difference:</p>

<ul>
  <li><strong>Pick one main action per page.</strong> Call, book, request a quote or order. Too many choices makes people choose nothing.</li>
  <li><strong>Make the button say what happens.</strong> "Request a quote" or "Schedule a consultation" beats "Submit" or "Learn more."</li>
  <li><strong>Repeat the action.</strong> Put it near the top, after key sections and at the bottom, so it's there when someone is ready.</li>
  <li><strong>Answer the worries.</strong> A short FAQ covering timing, service area and what to expect clears up the doubts that stop people from reaching out.</li>
  <li><strong>Make contact easy.</strong> A tap-to-call phone number and a short form work better on phones than a long questionnaire.</li>
</ul>

<h2>A quick checklist before you publish</h2>

<ul>
  <li>Does the page answer one main search, and is it different from every other page on your site?</li>
  <li>Is that search in the title, the headline area and the first paragraph?</li>
  <li>Would a stranger know what you do, who it's for and where you work within five seconds?</li>
  <li>Do the headings sound like questions people really ask?</li>
  <li>Is there at least one specific detail or piece of proof only your business could share?</li>
  <li>Is there one clear next step, repeated where it helps?</li>
  <li>Does it read well on a phone?</li>
</ul>

<p>If you're rebuilding your whole site, our <a href="/blog/website-redesign-checklist-new-mexico">website redesign checklist</a> walks through the bigger picture, from structure to launch.</p>

